  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1255 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nCurrent satellite imagery shows extensive cloud cover over north\nMississippi and far southwest Tennessee with mainly clear skies\nover the remainder of Tennessee, east Arkansas and the Missouri\nBootheel.  Temperatures are in the 70s across the area, but\ncooler temperatures are expected tomorrow.\n\nA 1004 mb surface low is approaching western Ohio with an\nattendant cold front stretches south and west across the Missouri\nBootheel into east Arkansas. Ahead of the front, weak WAA is\nproducing scattered showers over north Mississippi.  No lightning\nhas been observed so far today, but a few strikes are possible as\ninstability associated with daytime heating increases.  There is\na marginal risk of severe storms this afternoon near the\nTennessee River.  Clear skies in this area will allow for maximum\ninstability, but given dew points in the low to middle 50s CAPE\nshould remain below350 J/kg.  40-50kt of shear will shift into\nnorthwest Tennessee later this evening, but likely too late to\ncoincide with the thermodynamic instability.  Nevertheless, there\nis a chance for a strong to severe storm this evening as storms\nmove into Middle Tennessee.  North winds will arrive in the\nMidsouth tonight resulting in cooler temperatures for the\nremainder of the week into the weekend.\n\nA narrow but strong ridge will build over the Rockies and shift\nover the Plains tomorrow into Friday.  Strong northwest flow will\nset up along the Mississippi River Valley. A reinforcing cold\nfront will move across the Mid-South Thursday night into  Friday.\n Temperatures behind this front will be the coldest so far this\nyear, featuring lows in the middle 20s to middle 30s Saturday and\nSunday. Weekend high temperatures will be mostly in the 50s, but\nportions of northwest Tennessee may struggle to climb out of the\n40s Saturday afternoon.\n\nModel guidance is in very poor agreement after the weekend.  The\ngeneral upper level pattern will consist of a broad ridge across\nthe Plains and Mississippi River Valley.  The ECMWF features a\ncutoff low over east Texas, early Monday, shifting across\nMississippi and Alabama before midweek.  The GFS has a weaker low\nover Ohio, sinking south across West Virginia. The former\nsolution would result in a comparatively wetter and cooler\npattern across the Mid-South, but not as cold as the weekend. The\nNBM is a reasonable compromise between the other two solutions.\nTemperatures will trend a bit warmer with only a slight chance of\nrainfall. By Tuesday, high temperatures in northeast Mississippi\nmay rebound back into the low 60s.",
